 The Isotropic Disney BRDF is a simplified material model based on the Cook-Torrance microfacet framework, designed to model reflectance for both metals and plastics in a unified manner. It is a restricted subset of the full Disney Principled BRDF, optimized for differentiable rendering and optimization tasks where ambiguity from high-dimensional parameter spaces must be reduced. Unlike the full model, it restricts optimization to opaque surfaces with spatially varying diffuse albedo and global constants for roughness, specular albedo, and metalness.
 
+### Diffuse ABSDF Factorization
+A sub-component of the ABSDF framework that handles the analytical integration of the diffuse term. It assumes $\beta_c$ and $\beta_m$ are orientation-independent, allowing the joint parameter PDF to split into marginal PDFs. The integral $\int_{[0,1]^2} (1 - \beta_m) \beta_c p(\beta_c, \beta_m) d\beta_c d\beta_m$ simplifies to $E[\beta_c] - E[\beta_m \beta_c]$ [S1, unofficial]. For orientation-varying parameters, the clamped dot product function $\langle n \cdot \omega_i \rangle \langle n \cdot \omega_o \rangle$ is approximated by a Spherical Gaussian (SG) $c \cdot SG(\omega_h; n, \kappa)$, reducing the problem to an SG-convolution [S1, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Complexity:** The base evaluation is $O(1)$ per sample. Aggregation via ABSDF factorization involves convolutions of Spherical Gaussians, which depend on the number of basis functions used to approximate the parameter distributions.
-- **Performance:** Faster than the full Disney BRDF due to the omission of sheen and clearcoat lobes and the use of Lambertian diffuse [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple parameterization, analytical tractability for aggregation, wide applicability to standard PBR assets.
-- **Limitations:** Lacks detailed surface features like sheen and clearcoat; Lambertian diffuse is less accurate for non-Lambertian materials compared to the original Disney model.
+*   **Complexity:** The base evaluation is $O(1)$ per sample. The ABSDF factorization allows for pre-computation of moments, reducing runtime aggregation costs for level-of-detail rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simplicity compared to the full Disney BRDF; compatibility with analytical factorization techniques; wide applicability to standard PBR assets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lack of sheen and clearcoat lobes limits the ability to model certain materials (e.g., hair, varnished wood) accurately [S1, unofficial]. The Lambertian diffuse approximation may not capture subsurface scattering effects or retro-reflection present in the original model [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When using the ABSDF framework for efficient scene aggregation or when a simplified, robust PBR model is needed for differentiable rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Modified Disney Principled BRDF originates from the ABSDF (Efficient Scene Appearance Aggregation for Level-of-Detail Rendering) framework [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to support the widest applicability by targeting the Disney Principled BRDF [Burley 2012], one of the most commonly used models in production. The modifications were made to enable efficient analytical integration for level-of-detail rendering.
+The Modified Disney Principled BRDF was introduced in the context of the Efficient Scene Appearance Aggregation for Level-of-Detail Rendering paper [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the Disney Principled BRDF [Burley 2012], which is one of the most commonly used models in production for its versatility [S1, unofficial]. The modifications were made to facilitate the mathematical factorization required for the ABSDF method, specifically by simplifying the diffuse term and removing complex lobes [S1, unofficial].
 
